ings that a cell is  capable of doing  this information is necessarily  incomplete and so one of our goals was  really to  complement what we had learned and what  the field had learned  at the dna and rna level to better  understand  beyond copy number analysis sequence  analysis  and transcript analysis some of the  things that we really wanted to see  were how cells are dynamically  responding to their neighbors and what  their signaling capability at the  protein level  shown here really is so  many of the things that i'm going to  show you some of the important features  for us  in identifying cells that were of  interest are things like  signaling cascades so phosphorylated  signaling proteins which are shown here  in green with a red modification and so  as you'll see many of the stories that  we're interested in center around things  that need to be measured at the protein  level  in my lab we are particularly interested  in studying brain tumors  and in the research i'm going to tell  you about today we have a special focus  in the most malignant form of primary  brain tumor glioblastoma  so glioblastomas are by far the most  common  tumors that present in the brain that  have  origins within the brain they're about  half of all brain tumors and the huge  majority  of malignant brain tumors  unfortunately although they've been a  heavy focus of study  for many years glioblastoma prognosis  has not improved much  over the past decades what i'm showing  you at the left here is the last major  change to standard of care  for these tumors the introduction of the  alkylating agent temozolomide  in addition to radiotherapy and while  use of this agent does somewhat extend  survival as shown in  this kaplan-meier plot on the left you  can see  that this curve still trends downward  and so many patients have a very poor  prognosis and there's been a huge effort  to try to better understand these tumors  so that we can come up with new ways of  targeting them  in particular there's been an extensive  amount of work to profile dna and rna  changes within these tumors resulting in  their organization into molecular  subclasses that are thought to reflect  some of the  underlying biology in different patients  tumors  and so there are currently three major  uh  subtypes of glioblastoma they're  distinguished majorly  primarily by alterations in growth  factor receptor  gene copy number as well as  transcriptional signatures  that resemble different types of  progenitor cells within the brain  however one ongoing challenge in the  field  is that as illustrated by the smaller  survival plot in the bottom right  there are not large differences yet in  clinical outcome between patients  that have these different subtypes of  glioblastoma  so more work needs to be done to  understand how we can couple  these biological variations and  different subclasses  to features that might be targetable and  might  improve outcome and there are several  reasons why it's been a challenge to  transition these classifications into  understanding that we can use to design  new therapies  uh one of them is a problem that is  common to many solid tumors  glioblastomas as illustrated here in the  cartoon  are complex tumors so they have many um  different cancer lineage cells within  them  cells that uh resemble different stages  of neural stem cell differentiation but  have cancer lineage mutations in them  these tumors are also highly  infiltrative and so there are many  normal cells  within any resected tumor mass that we  study for example  um neurons astrocytes and glia the  resonant cells of the brain there are  also endothelial cells these  tumors are noted to have abnormal  vasculature  and importantly there are many different  types of infiltrating immune cells  and so many of the analyses that i  referenced before  use bulk dna or rna profiling which  while informative  are difficult in allowing us to see the  cell to cell heterogeneity  within these masses and this is perhaps  easily illustrated in one example piece  of data from our group  so these are cells from a glioblastoma  tumor  which underwent copy number alteration  analysis so this would be considered  potentially  a classical subtype of glioblastoma  and based primarily on having an  estimated copy number of the egfr  gene of 22 versus the normally expected  two however when we look at this biaxial  plot of s100b  and astrocyte marker and egfr the  protein that we would expect to be  highly expressed across this tumor  when you look at this contour plot of  all of the events where we measured egfr  protein expression on the cell surface  what i hope you can see is that although  there are certainly some cells that  express egfr protein  a huge majority of cells in the tumor  actually don't have  very high protein level expression and  so again  copying number alterations while  informative and helping us think about  the biology of the tumor  haven't told us the whole story yet  so some of the things that we are  interested in understanding in my lab  and the reason that we developed the  tools i'm going to introduce  today are that we wanted to understand  these heterogeneous  mixtures of cells and also how they vary  across patients and so one of the  driving questions that  has interested my lab and collaborators  over the last few years  are one how can we find highly malignant  phenotypes across glioblastoma  so how can we find the most aggressive  cells the ones that are driving  recurrence which might not be the  majority of the tumor mass  and whose abundance might vary across  patients  and also how can we do discovery work in  smaller cohorts of patients so for  example clinical trials  might require hundreds of patients but  for  discovery work in biology we might need  to work with smaller groups of patients  in the study i'm going to show you we  looked at a discovery cohort  of 28 and so how can we leverage  all of the information that we get from  approaches like mass cytometry where we  have millions of cells  and lots of data on specific parameters  that we measured  to figure out which of these parameters  help us find  the most bad actors the cells that are  driving the worst outcomes  and then identify their targetable  features  so the approach that we elected to use  as a complement to many studies using  single cell sequencing of the dna and  rna levels  is mass cytometry or cytosk  and one of the reasons that we were  particularly excited to use this is that  it allows us to measure many features  we selected a panel of about 40  different parameters  based on our knowledge of brain tumor  biology and some of the  previous genetic analyses in the field  and  it lets us look at millions of cells so  we're really well powered  to try to find rare subsets that might  be a minority  of the primary tumor when it comes from  the operating room to our lab  so in this cartoon schematic i'm showing  you a general workflow that we developed  and published together with our  collaborators in the irish lab at  vanderbilt  in the study i'm talking about today all  of the patients had  idh wild-type glioblastoma the most  common molecular classification of  glioblastoma  and all of these are primary tumors  meaning that this is before  this is from the initial resection  before patients undergo temozolomide and  radiation therapy  so when tumors are resected in the  operating room  within an hour they come to our  laboratory and are  viably dissociated and viably  cryopreserved  using protocols that we have since  published so that we have millions of  cells  archived in liquid nitrogen that we can  then  bring out and study through  immunostaining and mass cytometry  so we first developed a panel of  antibodies to allow us to find  immune endothelial and other  infiltrating cells and then to focus in  on the presumed cancer lineage cells  that are the majority of the cells  in these samples