 for starter cohorts  and so you take the fcs files or the i'm  the imd files and um  feed the data the data into rapid  together  with the in this case uh survival  uh times for all of the patients so you  start without knowing for example which  patients were good or bad  outcome necessarily and then  most of the remaining steps are done in  an automated fashion  so the gnp and gpp phenotypes and then  the labels that define them  are generated as part of the rapid  workflow  and so the short answer is uh we did not  know  at the beginning um which  cell uh phenotypes and which features  were going to be the most important ones  and that's where having a lot of  parameters to be able to analyze was  really helpful because we were able to  cast a very  wide net for example um we might have  hypothesized based on the literature  that um expression of other stem-like  features like cd133 would be very  important  for identifying prognostically  meaningful cells but it turns out  we measured that along with eight other  you know things associated with stemness  and only sox2  was selectively enriched on the gnp  population so i hope that helps cancer  something about the cells and then  in terms of how patients for example are  assigned to  being high or low for a particular  self-phenotype so they have high  abundance or low abundance that is also  done  in a largely automated fashion based on  the distribution of abundances  within the cohort so again we try not to  create an artificial cut off we try to  find it in a statistically determined  way  okay thank you for that dr erie  next question since gnp  is inversely proportional to immune  cells does the same apply to gpp  in other words is immune cell enrichment  and tumors a good predictor of survival  if yes how do you explain mesence gbm's  survival which is heavily  infiltrated with immune cells right so  um i can see the speaker as familiar  with some of the gbm literature  so um let me answer the gbp  question first um so  yes gpp high patients have a somewhat  proportionately higher  immune infiltration and that's just  looking at total  cd45 positive cells um  so well it somewhat went along with  survival in our smaller discovery cohort  i think there's a more complex story  which you're getting at with the  question about glioblastomas that are  transcriptionally classified  as mesenchymal are thought to have a lot  of immune infiltration  and that's based partially i think on  immunostaining studies of tmas and  partially on transcript level  analysis um so i guess  what i would say and what i think some  of todd's data that i briefly covered  demonstrated is that although the the  proportion of immune cells in the tumor  potentially has a small uh and has some  influence on survival it is definitely  not the whole story  and so it's much more important what the  exact complement of immune cells within  the tumor  is and beyond that i would also say just  because  you know t cells or specific  subpopulations are present  does not actually guarantee that they  are capable of carrying out their  you know their normally intended  function so i think it's going to be  really important to look at the  um ability of those cells to actually  mount an anti-tumor response  and some of the signaling pathways that  would indicate that activation is  happening  um so i think while  cd45 positive gives us a clue that  something is going on  we we need to explore it more deeply so  okay and oh i'm sorry can i also just  that  the other thing to keep in mind is that  when we try to look for protein-level  proxies  of the transcriptomic classification um  so for example  you would use uh transcriptomic or  genomic alterations to call a tumor  as mesenchymal or another subclass and  we don't always see that that perfectly  matches  expression of those things like cd44 or  matte for the mesenchymal subclass  at the protein level and so i also think  um one of the things that will be really  interesting as we go forward  is to go back and retrospectively  use transcriptomic and genomic  classification on tumors that we know  have gnp  or gpp to try to figure out how those  categories fit together  so okay our next question  i see hla dr plus cd45 minus cells  what are those in tsne plots and cd64  plus cd4 plus cells can you please  comment on that  yes so todd the generator of that data  is actually in the audience and i have  asked him as well  so one thing that i think is important  to point out about the particular t-sne  plot in question  is that all of the events that are on  that plot have already been gated for  being cd45  positive i.e we've already pulled out  all of the immune cells and so  when we display the heat for 45  on that t-sne plot they're going to look  very low when in fact it's  that the range is uh much narrower than  you might think  so that particular population we think  is cd45 low hle dr  positive microglia and then on the other  side  the cd4 cd64 are we think are  macrophages  um and i'm sure todd would be happy to  talk about this more  offline if you would like okay great  next question is which is the experience  using any of these markers to follow  patients after the tumor has been  removed in order to check their  prognosis  so if we use any of these  to follow patients so all of the  analyses that i showed  in the rapid workflow are analyzing  tumors  at the initial resection so this is the  tumor is resected  then the patients in this case underwent  standard of care  treatment so we do have um  information both about time to  recurrence of the tumor so progression  free survival which i didn't have time  to talk  about as well as overall survival  so in our case and in what i was able to  show today  we were able to test  how well the combination of markers that  identify gnp and gpp cells  um predict um outcome for the patients  and when we did the tissue microarray  staining that i showed a little bit  later  the part of the point of that was to try  to say can we pull out some of the most  highly enriched markers and use them  to stratify outcome in an independent  cohort so trying to get more at this  predictive question  what i think will be really interesting  to do  going forward that would take this a  little further is to see  when you look at recurrent tumors  do they converge on say a more gnp-like  phenotype so can we learn something  about  what tumors look like post-treatment or  which of these populations are  especially  refractory to standard of care or  investigational treatments  i think that's what the questioner is  getting at but please let me know if you  have a follow up  okay we have time for one more question  how much time does it take to collect  data from a core using imc  so i i'm going to say that this actually  i  could probably talk about in the ask the  expert session this afternoon or dr  bowden miller cred if you don't mind i'd  like to answer the one after that i see  yes is rapid easily translatable to  other diseases and populations  and then a second question about can you  use it on single cell transcriptomics so  first we specifically designed rapid  because we wanted something that would  be translatable to other diseases and  cell populations  and in fact in the paper we actually  tested it on one of dr davis's  who spoke earlier this morning one of  her data sets and we're independently  able to  find the same some of the same  phenotypes that they had shown  where predictive outcomes so we know  that it can translate to other  cytoske data sets quite well and  the intent is that it should be is that  it is modular and can be applied to lots  of other  single cell data types where you have a  continuous measurement  that you want to that you want to use  that could be  patient outcome in days or weeks it  could be time to relapse  it could even be percent immune  infiltrate so the idea is that  um anything where you have that kind of  continuous data  and a sort of medium-sized  investigational single cell data set you  might be able to use this approach  okay thank you again dr erie for your  time and for your  important research before we go i'd like  to thank the audience for joining us  today  questions submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  as a reminder please visit the fluidine  booths in the exhibit hall to interact  with representatives  and gather collateral into your virtual  briefcases for later review  this webcast can be viewed on demand  through september of 2021.  until next time goodbye
 

